# P2-G6 NAMED FAULT MATRIX -- the upstream half.
#
# One fakesrv script arms EVERY named upstream fault the fake can express, each
# pinned to a distinct 1-based get occurrence (`on=get:<nth>`). The driver issues
# the gets in order, so get N triggers exactly the fault on row N of the matrix
# and nothing else -- a stable `site:nth` injection point (the plan's phrase),
# reproducible byte-for-byte because the fault is keyed to the request ORDINAL,
# never to a timer or a race.
#
# WHY THE FAKE UPSTREAM AND NOT A MODULE fault_set HOOK. The plan's boundary is
# "no generic nginx core fault without a consumer-owned assertion". Here the
# CONSUMER whose cleanup is under test is nginx's own ngx_http_upstream machinery
# -- ngx_http_upstream_finalize_request and the memcached upstream module -- which
# owns the per-request pool and the upstream connection on EVERY one of these
# error branches. The harness owns the assertion (fd + cycle-pool neutrality read
# from the probe), so the pair is complete without shipping a fault_set hook the
# ref module deliberately omits. The probe's fault_slab=/fault_palloc= sites (H1)
# need a real module fault site + a consumer .so; the upstream-fault matrix needs
# neither and therefore runs on the stock ref-probe CI legs.
#
# Gated by backend-smoke: a fault proves nothing about cleanup unless the same
# fake is first proven to serve correctly when told to behave normally. get 1
# below is that clean baseline get -- no fault on it.
proto   memcached
seed    k       matrix-baseline-value-0123456789abcdefghij

# --- the matrix rows, one named fault per get ordinal ----------------------
# CRITICAL: the driver issues WARMUP (8) CLEAN gets FIRST to settle the fd and
# cycle-pool baseline; those consume get ordinals 1..8. The faults must therefore
# start at ordinal 9, or they would fire DURING warmup and the matrix-loop gets
# would be unfaulted -- a silently vacuous scenario. The get-ordinal column in
# matrix.tsv is (WARMUP + row index) and the driver asserts each row's declared
# ordinal equals the get number it is about to issue, so a drift between this
# file's ordinals, the table and the driver's WARMUP reds the scenario rather
# than passing on unfaulted gets.
#
# Every row's action is one the memcached reply path can actually carry (a real
# command target accepts every action; validate_fault only restricts the
# reply-less connect/idle pseudo-targets), so none is silently dropped.
fault   on=get:9     action=rst
fault   on=get:10    action=truncate     after=8
fault   on=get:11    action=lie_bytes    delta=-20
fault   on=get:12    action=lie_bytes    delta=40
fault   on=get:13    action=drip         bytes=4     ms=50
fault   on=get:14    action=close_after  ms=10
